Cell[TextData[{
 "Their difference is the number of vertices of ",
 StyleBox["A", "TI"],
 " with a neighbour outside ",
 StyleBox["A", "TI"],
 ", the ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraBoundary"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraBoundary", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "d76e0a5b-2b11-4bb2-96f0-f9c871f0d9f2"],
 " of ",
 StyleBox["A", "TI"],
 "; ",
 StyleBox["A\[Degree]", "TI"],
 " is its ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TemplateBox[{
    Cell[
     TextData["InfraInterior"]], 
    "paclet:WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry/ref/InfraInterior", 
    "WolframInstitute/InfraGeometry Package Symbol"},
   "PackageLink",
   BaseStyle->"InlineFormula"]], "InlineFormula",ExpressionUUID->
  "f8a7b21f-c150-4675-a2e2-fad1bf272af2"],
 ". In the limit of a manifold the boundary of a region has measure zero and \
the two agree. On a graph they do not: the boundary of a ball of radius ",
 StyleBox["r", "TI"],
 " holds a share of order ",
 StyleBox["1/r", "TI"],
 " of its vertices. On the square, triangular and hexagonal lattices, away \
from the rim, the Riemannian measure of a ball of radius ",
 StyleBox["r", "TI"],
 " is the counting measure of the ball of radius ",
 StyleBox["r \[Minus] 1", "TI"],
 ", the convention of the Wolfram Physics technical introduction. The rim of \
a finite graph is not a boundary: a region that fills the graph has both \
measures equal to the number of vertices."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"2476ae88-9f94-4bfd-9978-0a6949f6a108"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "A Euclidean head is inert: it holds its points and options and computes \
nothing on its own. ",
 Cell[BoxData["InfraMeasurement"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "49cd6bf8-138d-40ff-825f-2821fd241448"],
 " is what evaluates it, reading every property off the head's ",
 StyleBox["graph",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " \[LongDash] an acyclic directed graph whose source-to-sink chains are \
exactly the head's members \[LongDash] by one forward and one backward sweep \
of a dynamic-programming count, never by enumeration. A circle's graph is a \
",
 Cell[BoxData["List"], "InlineFormula",
  FontSize->0.9 Inherited,ExpressionUUID->
  "609e969a-7a31-4741-aa2f-fcbf39b114b0"],
 " of necklaces, each ",
 StyleBox["opened",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 " at its closing arrow ",
 StyleBox["u", "TI"],
 " -> ",
 StyleBox["s1", "TI"],
 ": an acyclic DAG with one source ",
 StyleBox["s1", "TI"],
 " and one sink ",
 StyleBox["u", "TI"],
 ". A circle's member is the open chain ",
 StyleBox["s1", "TI"],
 " \[Ellipsis] ",
 StyleBox["u", "TI"],
 " read cyclically, a cyclic vertex list whose first vertex is not repeated."
}], "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"38f68e24-9351-4061-8786-532ad9a437ea"],

Cell["The properties:", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"a8063279-259e-4123-a14e-591e41cd38a3"],

